# Campaign Dossier — Hades Campaign

**Threat Level: HIGH | Attribution: Miasma (HIGH confidence) | Status: ACTIVE**

-----

## BLUF

The Hades Campaign is an actively-spreading software supply-chain compromise targeting Python developer environments, first publicly documented by StepSecurity on 2026-06-08. At least 29 PyPI packages — concentrated in the graph machine-learning, computational-biology, bioinformatics, and genotype-phenotype analysis ecosystems — were trojanized with an identical multi-layer payload that executes silently on package import. The campaign is assessed as the latest evolution of the **Miasma** threat actor (prior npm “Phantom Gyp” worm and Mini Shai-Hulud activity), reusing Miasma’s credential-harvesting, self-replicating worm logic, and GitHub-based exfiltration while introducing three notable advances: (1) **cross-platform in-memory secret scraping** on Linux, macOS, and Windows CI runners; (2) **adversarial prompt injection** designed to coerce LLM-based malware-triage pipelines into issuing false-clean verdicts; and (3) a **token-revocation wiper deterrent** that destroys host data if stolen credentials are revoked. The campaign self-propagates via SSH/SCP, GitHub Actions workflow injection, and abuse of PyPI/npm OIDC trusted publishing with forged Sigstore/SLSA provenance.

**Relevance to FRANK / Archimedes:** This campaign directly threatens the developer toolchain Ryan operates — Python package installs, GitHub PAT usage, Claude Code / AI-assistant config files, and the GitHub-API-based publishing workflow used for Archimedes and proxmox-lab. The wiper deterrent and AI-config backdoor vectors warrant immediate hygiene review. See Mitigations.

