## Attribution

- **Tracked actor:** Miasma (StepSecurity naming)
- **Confidence:** HIGH — core TTPs (multi-cloud credential sweep, self-spreading worm logic, GitHub-based exfiltration via commit dead-drops, Runner.Worker memory reads) are directly continuous with previously documented Miasma npm campaigns (“Phantom Gyp” binding.gyp worm; Microsoft Azure repo compromise; Mini Shai-Hulud Linux memory-read variant).
- **Naming convention shift:** Exfiltration artifacts moved from Dune-themed terms (prior Miasma/Shai-Hulud campaigns) to Underworld/Hades-themed terms in this wave — an actor signature change, not a separate group.
- **Note on overlapping reporting:** Socket Research Team documented a parallel “Hades wave” of a Mini Shai-Hulud variant (37 malicious PyPI wheels across 19 packages) without attributing to a specific group. Some vendors have previously linked Mini Shai-Hulud activity to TeamPCP (financially motivated). Treat cross-vendor package counts as partially overlapping, not additive.

-----

## Targeting

- **Primary ecosystem:** PyPI — graph ML, computational biology, bioinformatics, genotype-phenotype analysis packages.
- **Victim profile:** Developers and CI/CD pipelines that import the trojanized packages; downstream organizations whose GitHub Actions runners and publishing credentials are reachable from a compromised dependency.
- **Secondary spread:** Any host reachable via a compromised developer’s SSH known_hosts/config; any repo where a harvested GitHub token has write or workflow scope.

